Spring Boot auto‑configuration is a feature that automatically wires common Spring components based on the libraries present on the class‑path. It uses conditional annotations (e.g., @ConditionalOnClass, @ConditionalOnMissingBean) so that a bean is created only when the required classes are available and no user‑defined bean of the same type exists. Starters bundle a set of auto‑configurations that are enabled via spring.factories.

**Demo example** – a minimal JPA‑based application that shows auto‑configuration for a data source, JPA, and a REST endpoint:

1. **pom.xml**
```xml
<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
        <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
        <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```
2. **application.yml**
```yaml
spring:
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:h2:mem:testdb
    driver-class-name: org.h2.Driver
    username: sa
    password:
  jpa:
    hibernate:
      ddl-auto: create-drop
    show-sql: true
```
3. **User entity**
```java
@Entity
public class User {
    @Id @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;
    private String name;
    private String email;
    // getters & setters
}
```
4. **Repository**
```java
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    List<User> findByNameContainingIgnoreCase(String name);
}
```
5. **Service**
```java
@Service
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repo;
    public UserService(UserRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }
    public List<User> search(String q) { return repo.findByNameContainingIgnoreCase(q); }
    public User create(User u) { return repo.save(u); }
}
```
6. **Controller**
```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/users")
public class UserController {
    private final UserService service;
    public UserController(UserService service) { this.service = service; }
    @PostMapping public User add(@RequestBody User user) { return service.create(user); }
    @GetMapping("/search") public List<User> search(@RequestParam String q) { return service.search(q); }
}
```
7. **Main application**
```java
@SpringBootApplication
public class DemoApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class, args);
    }
}
```

**What happens under the hood?**
- `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa` pulls in `spring-boot-autoconfigure`.
- `DataSourceAutoConfiguration` sees the H2 driver and creates a `DataSource`.
- `HibernateJpaAutoConfiguration` creates an `EntityManagerFactory` and `JpaTransactionManager`.
- `RepositoryAutoConfiguration` creates the concrete implementation of `UserRepository`.
- Component scanning registers `@Service`, `@Repository`, and `@RestController` beans.

**Query examples**
- Create user: `POST /api/users` with JSON body `{"name":"Alice","email":"alice@example.com"}`.
- Search users: `GET /api/users/search?q=ali`.

**Overriding defaults** – declare your own bean of the same type; Spring Boot will skip the auto‑configured one due to `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`.

Spring Boot auto‑configuration lets you focus on business logic while the framework handles plumbing such as data sources, JPA, JDBC templates, and embedded servers.

