### 1. What is Spring Boot Auto‑Configuration?

Spring Boot’s auto‑configuration mechanism automatically wires up common Spring components based on the libraries that are on the class‑path.  
* **No manual bean definitions** – the framework supplies beans such as `DataSource`, `JdbcTemplate`, `EntityManagerFactory`, etc.  
* **Conditional** – a bean is created only if the required classes are present and no bean of the same type has already been defined (`@ConditionalOnClass`, `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`).  
* **Starter‑based** – each starter (e.g. `spring‑boot‑starter‑data‑jpa`) bundles a set of auto‑configurations that are enabled via the `spring.factories` file.

> **Key point** – the goal is to let you write *just the business logic* while the framework takes care of the plumbing.

---

### 2. Demo Code

Below is a minimal Spring Boot application that demonstrates auto‑configuration for a JPA‑based data source and a REST endpoint.

#### 2.1. `pom.xml`

```xml
<dependencies>
    <!-- Spring Boot Starters -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- H2 in‑memory DB for demo -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
        <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
        <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

#### 2.2. `application.yml`

```yaml
spring:
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:h2:mem:testdb
    driver-class-name: org.h2.Driver
    username: sa
    password:
  jpa:
    hibernate:
      ddl-auto: create-drop   # create schema on startup, drop on shutdown
    show-sql: true
```

#### 2.3. `User` Entity

```java
@Entity
public class User {
    @Id @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;
    private String name;
    private String email;
    // getters & setters omitted for brevity
}
```

#### 2.4. Repository

```java
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    List<User> findByNameContainingIgnoreCase(String name);
}
```

#### 2.5. Service

```java
@Service
public class UserService {

    private final UserRepository repo;

    public UserService(UserRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }

    public List<User> search(String query) {
        return repo.findByNameContainingIgnoreCase(query);
    }

    public User create(User user) {
        return repo.save(user);
    }
}
```

#### 2.6. REST Controller

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/users")
public class UserController {

    private final UserService service;

    public UserController(UserService service) {
        this.service = service;
    }

    @PostMapping
    public User add(@RequestBody User user) {
        return service.create(user);
    }

    @GetMapping("/search")
    public List<User> search(@RequestParam String q) {
        return service.search(q);
    }
}
```

#### 2.7. Main Application

```java
@SpringBootApplication
public class DemoApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class, args);
    }
}
```

> **What happens under the hood?**  
> * `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa` pulls in `spring-boot-autoconfigure`.  
> * `DataSourceAutoConfiguration` sees the H2 driver on the class‑path and creates a `DataSource`.  
> * `HibernateJpaAutoConfiguration` creates an `EntityManagerFactory` and `JpaTransactionManager`.  
> * `JpaRepositoryFactoryBean` creates the concrete implementation of `UserRepository`.  
> * The `@SpringBootApplication` annotation enables component scanning, so all `@Service`, `@Repository`, and `@RestController` beans are registered automatically.

---

### 3. Query Examples

Using the REST API built above, you can perform the following operations:

| HTTP Method | Endpoint | Purpose | Example |
|-------------|----------|---------|---------|
| `POST /api/users` | Create a new user | `{"name":"Alice","email":"alice@example.com"}` | `curl -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"name":"Alice","email":"alice@example.com"}' http://localhost:8080/api/users` |
| `GET /api/users/search?q=ali` | Search users by name (case‑insensitive) | Returns all users whose name contains “ali” | `curl http://localhost:8080/api/users/search?q=ali` |

#### Behind the scenes – JPA query

```java
List<User> findByNameContainingIgnoreCase(String name);
```

Spring Data JPA parses the method name, creates a JPQL query equivalent to:

```sql
SELECT u FROM User u WHERE LOWER(u.name) LIKE LOWER(CONCAT('%', :name, '%'))
```

If you prefer custom JPQL:

```java
@Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.email = :email")
Optional<User> findByEmail(@Param("email") String email);
```

---

### 4. Recap

| Feature | Auto‑Configured By | What You Get |
|---------|--------------------|--------------|
| `DataSource` | `DataSourceAutoConfiguration` | JDBC connection to H2 (or any DB) |
| `EntityManagerFactory` | `HibernateJpaAutoConfiguration` | ORM layer |
| `JdbcTemplate` | `JdbcTemplateAutoConfiguration` | Simple JDBC helper |
| `JpaRepository` implementations | `RepositoryAutoConfiguration` | CRUD + query derivation |
| Embedded Tomcat | `EmbeddedServletContainerAutoConfiguration` | Runs the app on port 8080 by default |

With these pieces automatically wired, you can focus on writing domain logic, writing tests, and iterating quickly. If you ever need to override a default, simply declare a bean of the same type in your own configuration – Spring Boot will skip the auto‑configured one because of `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`.

